84
00:04:25,890 --> 00:04:28,690
So the three paradigms
that I'm talking about here

85
00:04:28,690 --> 00:04:30,610
are pre-training,
which is basically

86
00:04:30,610 --> 00:04:34,370
training the model on
internet that we have talked

87
00:04:34,370 --> 00:04:35,790
about in the first lecture.

88
00:04:35,790 --> 00:04:38,050
And we're not covering
that much in detail.

89
00:04:38,050 --> 00:04:40,990
And then the next technique
would be that fine-tuning,

90
00:04:40,990 --> 00:04:42,670
which is how you
get your chatbots,

91
00:04:42,670 --> 00:04:44,930
where you're basically
perhaps fine-tuning or doing

92
00:04:44,930 --> 00:04:46,770
reinforcement learning
with human feedback

93
00:04:46,770 --> 00:04:51,748
or RLAIF on preference
data from humans.

94
00:04:51,748 --> 00:04:53,290
And then the test
time scaling, which

95
00:04:53,290 --> 00:04:54,790
was some of the
techniques that were

96
00:04:54,790 --> 00:04:56,610
covered in previous lectures.

97
00:04:56,610 --> 00:04:58,610
Those are more techniques
where you're basically

98
00:04:58,610 --> 00:05:02,530
doing some sort of say, majority
voting or inference time

99
00:05:02,530 --> 00:05:04,370
sampling, and then
combining the outputs

100
00:05:04,370 --> 00:05:07,610
from how you sample
from the model.

101
00:05:07,610 --> 00:05:11,750
For example, in homework 1, you
are evaluating majority voting.

102
00:05:11,750 --> 00:05:14,130
Then you are evaluating
some other techniques

103
00:05:14,130 --> 00:05:18,570
that combine and evaluate the
errors in what the model is

104
00:05:18,570 --> 00:05:21,190
producing and then using
that to improve the model.

105
00:05:21,190 --> 00:05:24,050
So test time scaling is mostly
inference-based techniques.

106
00:05:24,050 --> 00:05:27,410
Now, if you take what
the model output filter

107
00:05:27,410 --> 00:05:29,810
after applying this
test time scaling

108
00:05:29,810 --> 00:05:32,370
and then use that to
fine-tune further,

109
00:05:32,370 --> 00:05:34,222
that's roughly what
train time scaling is.

110
00:05:34,222 --> 00:05:36,430
So if you take nothing away
from this entire lecture,

111
00:05:36,430 --> 00:05:38,690
but you just remember
this loop, then

112
00:05:38,690 --> 00:05:43,210
you have learned the basics of
what train time scaling does.

