145
00:07:17,820 --> 00:07:19,340
on this particular benchmark.

146
00:07:19,340 --> 00:07:20,680
Now, why does this work?

147
00:07:20,680 --> 00:07:23,320
So math is a domain in which
there is verifiability.

148
00:07:23,320 --> 00:07:26,400
So we had a whole lecture
on robust verification.

149
00:07:26,400 --> 00:07:28,908
So you have the ability to
know which outputs are correct,

150
00:07:28,908 --> 00:07:30,200
and which ones are not correct.

151
00:07:30,200 --> 00:07:32,380
So you have some ability
to actually choose

152
00:07:32,380 --> 00:07:34,300
the correct outputs
that can then

153
00:07:34,300 --> 00:07:35,920
feed into train time scaling.

154
00:07:35,920 --> 00:07:40,140
So this works better in domains
where there is verifiability.

155
00:07:40,140 --> 00:07:44,940
So typically, why does
reasoning start to help?

156
00:07:44,940 --> 00:07:47,160
So this is a repeat
of some of the slides,

157
00:07:47,160 --> 00:07:49,180
but I think it's very
important to remind

158
00:07:49,180 --> 00:07:52,500
folks that all the reasoning
models in all series

159
00:07:52,500 --> 00:07:55,500
are of Gemini flash
thinking, or the equivalents

160
00:07:55,500 --> 00:07:57,980
in other frontier labs.

161
00:07:57,980 --> 00:08:00,920
Basically, reasoning allows us
to solve for difficult problems,

162
00:08:00,920 --> 00:08:02,920
because when you're solving
a difficult problem,

163
00:08:02,920 --> 00:08:04,760
you will not just
spit out the output,

164
00:08:04,760 --> 00:08:06,832
even in chain of thought
and math problems.

165
00:08:06,832 --> 00:08:09,040
When you were asking the
model to think step by step,

166
00:08:09,040 --> 00:08:10,582
it allowed a certain
amount of tokens

167
00:08:10,582 --> 00:08:14,020
to be dedicated to
spelling out the reasoning

168
00:08:14,020 --> 00:08:15,560
and step by step process.

169
00:08:15,560 --> 00:08:17,300
So thinking models
use a chain of thought

170
00:08:17,300 --> 00:08:20,000
where when they're attempting
to solve the problem,

171
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:22,580
and what these tokens
might get dedicated to is,

172
00:08:22,580 --> 00:08:24,880
you will see patterns
like problem analysis,

173
00:08:24,880 --> 00:08:26,900
where they might actually
try to understand

174
00:08:26,900 --> 00:08:28,900
what the problem is describing.

175
00:08:28,900 --> 00:08:30,380
Task decomposition.

176
00:08:30,380 --> 00:08:32,820
I'll show you an
example of this where

177
00:08:32,820 --> 00:08:36,539
they try to decompose
the task into sub-tasks.

178
00:08:36,539 --> 00:08:37,840
Self-evaluation.

179
00:08:37,840 --> 00:08:40,299
They might actually try
to look at the answer,

180
00:08:40,299 --> 00:08:41,580
and then be like, OK.

181
00:08:41,580 --> 00:08:43,580
This is not looking correct.

182
00:08:43,580 --> 00:08:47,990
So they might actually choose
to backtrack and recognize

183
00:08:47,990 --> 00:08:50,430
their own mistakes, and then
backtrack to a previous step

184
00:08:50,430 --> 00:08:52,390
where they redo the problem.

185
00:08:52,390 --> 00:08:57,570
And they might try some kind
of different approaches.

186
00:08:57,570 --> 00:08:59,508
So it's like a somewhat
parallel search

187
00:08:59,508 --> 00:09:01,550
where they might actually
try multiple approaches

188
00:09:01,550 --> 00:09:04,110
at the same time, instead
of if the current one is not

189
00:09:04,110 --> 00:09:05,233
leading to a solution.

190
00:09:05,233 --> 00:09:07,150
So it needs to have some
sense of whether it's

191
00:09:07,150 --> 00:09:08,790
the correct solution or not.

192
00:09:08,790 --> 00:09:12,750
So here are some
examples from actually

193
00:09:12,750 --> 00:09:16,350
a earlier version of
the model in o1 series.

194
00:09:16,350 --> 00:09:19,710
So here, the user is
requesting a batch script,

195
00:09:19,710 --> 00:09:21,990
where it wants to understand--

196
00:09:21,990 --> 00:09:23,510
it's requesting a
batch script that

197
00:09:23,510 --> 00:09:25,990
will represent a
string with this format

198
00:09:25,990 --> 00:09:28,330
and print out the transpose
in the same format.

199
00:09:28,330 --> 00:09:32,310
So it's taking a matrix
and printing the transpose.

200
00:09:32,310 --> 00:09:37,150
And what you see in the
output is that the model first

201
00:09:37,150 --> 00:09:40,590
tries to understand what
the user is asking in terms

202
00:09:40,590 --> 00:09:42,510
of input and output
formats before it

203
00:09:42,510 --> 00:09:44,010
tries to solve the problem.

204
00:09:44,010 --> 00:09:47,670
So that's more of a problem
analysis kind of pattern.

205
00:09:47,670 --> 00:09:49,910
Another example for the
same problem you see

206
00:09:49,910 --> 00:09:52,410
is that it tries to
decompose the problem.

207
00:09:52,410 --> 00:09:54,110
So it will actually--
once it understood

208
00:09:54,110 --> 00:09:56,950
the problem, it's saying that
the approach should involve--

209
00:09:56,950 --> 00:09:59,870
maybe I should parse the input
string, then build a matrix,

210
00:09:59,870 --> 00:10:02,030
transpose the matrix, and
then output the matrix

211
00:10:02,030 --> 00:10:03,250
in the same format.

212
00:10:03,250 --> 00:10:06,030
So it's breaking it
into multiple steps.

213
00:10:06,030 --> 00:10:08,490
For this particular problem,
this might be an overkill,

214
00:10:08,490 --> 00:10:10,030
but in more complex problems.

215
00:10:10,030 --> 00:10:11,775
This might be a
better way to solve

216
00:10:11,775 --> 00:10:13,150
the problem because
you decompose

217
00:10:13,150 --> 00:10:15,590
the problem into smaller steps.

218
00:10:15,590 --> 00:10:17,513
And then self-correction.

219
00:10:17,513 --> 00:10:19,430
So this is a different
problem from chemistry,

220
00:10:19,430 --> 00:10:23,950
where it's asking pH of
some solution on a chemical

221
00:10:23,950 --> 00:10:28,770
and the model starts to
compute the pH value,

222
00:10:28,770 --> 00:10:32,210
but then it suddenly
has this notion of wait,

223
00:10:32,210 --> 00:10:33,690
the correct formula
is different.

224
00:10:33,690 --> 00:10:35,670
So it basically goes
and changes the formula

225
00:10:35,670 --> 00:10:37,130
that it was going to use.

226
00:10:37,130 --> 00:10:39,470
So even though it's
pulling from the knowledge

227
00:10:39,470 --> 00:10:42,970
that the model already has,
it's actually correcting itself.

228
00:10:42,970 --> 00:10:45,950
So it has the capability
of self-correction.

229
00:10:45,950 --> 00:10:48,050
And how does this
help the model?

230
00:10:48,050 --> 00:10:50,070
So as I was trying
to explain before,

231
00:10:50,070 --> 00:10:52,790
in domains where you have
verification like computer

232
00:10:52,790 --> 00:10:57,890
programming or data analysis
or mathematical calculation,

