355
00:16:24,750 --> 00:16:27,450
So we already
covered this earlier,

356
00:16:27,450 --> 00:16:30,130
and we showed this
in the first lecture.

357
00:16:30,130 --> 00:16:32,370
So chain-of-thought in
large language models

358
00:16:32,370 --> 00:16:34,490
provides both
interpretability, and if you

359
00:16:34,490 --> 00:16:37,490
ask the model to show
its work step by step,

360
00:16:37,490 --> 00:16:40,290
then provides boost
mathematical reasoning.

361
00:16:40,290 --> 00:16:42,690
And we saw several benchmarks
common sense reasoning

362
00:16:42,690 --> 00:16:43,770
and so on.

363
00:16:43,770 --> 00:16:45,890
So we want to
basically get the model

364
00:16:45,890 --> 00:16:49,250
to have strong step-by-step
reasoning capabilities

365
00:16:49,250 --> 00:16:52,050
along solving any problem.

366
00:16:52,050 --> 00:16:54,950
What is challenging with
existing approaches,

367
00:16:54,950 --> 00:16:57,390
if you were to try to get
this in existing approaches,

368
00:16:57,390 --> 00:16:59,810
so no internet scale data
has really reasoning steps

369
00:16:59,810 --> 00:17:00,830
most of the time.

370
00:17:00,830 --> 00:17:02,288
There are very few
internet sources

371
00:17:02,288 --> 00:17:04,690
that have the large-scale
reasoning steps.

372
00:17:04,690 --> 00:17:07,790
And then if you try to manually
annotate reasoning steps,

373
00:17:07,790 --> 00:17:09,849
that's very expensive
because you literally

374
00:17:09,849 --> 00:17:12,630
need humans to annotate
those reasoning steps.

375
00:17:12,630 --> 00:17:16,609
And if you try to automate this
process of generating reasoning

376
00:17:16,609 --> 00:17:18,790
based on known
solution patterns,

377
00:17:18,790 --> 00:17:23,770
then that also works only in
very, very specific domains.

378
00:17:23,770 --> 00:17:26,890
And then if you try to few-shot
prompt-- few-shot prompting

379
00:17:26,890 --> 00:17:30,150
means if you give it a few
examples of here is a problem,

380
00:17:30,150 --> 00:17:31,810
here is a reasoning
chain, here is

381
00:17:31,810 --> 00:17:34,890
an answer, then that
still underperforms

382
00:17:34,890 --> 00:17:38,030
models that can be fine tuned
on just larger data sets.

383
00:17:38,030 --> 00:17:42,050
So basically, small amount
of reasoning examples

384
00:17:42,050 --> 00:17:43,970
don't quite cut it,
and you can just

385
00:17:43,970 --> 00:17:46,530
have a larger data set
without reasoning chains.

386
00:17:46,530 --> 00:17:53,570
So the key insight that
STaR has is very simple.

387
00:17:53,570 --> 00:17:54,910
This is a very simple example.

388
00:17:54,910 --> 00:17:57,630
It will start with a
small number of examples,

389
00:17:57,630 --> 00:17:59,470
that is reasoning steps.

390
00:17:59,470 --> 00:18:01,170
It will generate solutions.

391
00:18:01,170 --> 00:18:02,890
So say 10k problems.

392
00:18:02,890 --> 00:18:05,270
10k is a representative number.

393
00:18:05,270 --> 00:18:08,330
And then it will only keep the
ones that have correct answers,

394
00:18:08,330 --> 00:18:11,290
but it will generate these
problems with rationale.

395
00:18:11,290 --> 00:18:13,590
And it can fine tune on
those, and it can repeat.

396
00:18:13,590 --> 00:18:14,790
So it can repeat this loop.

397
00:18:14,790 --> 00:18:16,993
So this what's new
here compared to what

398
00:18:16,993 --> 00:18:18,410
we were doing in
test time scaling

399
00:18:18,410 --> 00:18:21,210
is it's only keeping the ones
that have correct answers.

400
00:18:21,210 --> 00:18:23,683
Now, why did it not
produce correct answers

401
00:18:23,683 --> 00:18:25,100
on the other ones,
and what can we

402
00:18:25,100 --> 00:18:27,200
do with those particular
set of examples?

403
00:18:27,200 --> 00:18:29,340
So that's the other
interesting bit.

404
00:18:29,340 --> 00:18:32,200
So in certain problems where
it could not solve the problem,

405
00:18:32,200 --> 00:18:33,160
the model got stuck.

406
00:18:33,160 --> 00:18:37,180
So what we want to do is we
basically, if we just fine

407
00:18:37,180 --> 00:18:39,380
tune on the correct
examples, the model

408
00:18:39,380 --> 00:18:42,860
is not able to learn how
to solve new problems.

409
00:18:42,860 --> 00:18:45,340
Let me cover this slide,
and then we can come back.

410
00:18:45,340 --> 00:18:47,560
So there's basically no signal.

411
00:18:47,560 --> 00:18:50,220
So what we do is we give
the model the answer

412
00:18:50,220 --> 00:18:51,840
and ask it to explain backwards.

413
00:18:51,840 --> 00:18:55,180
So it's almost like I have
this question, I ask the model

414
00:18:55,180 --> 00:18:57,275
to produce the answer,
but now the model did not

415
00:18:57,275 --> 00:18:58,400
produce the correct answer.

416
00:18:58,400 --> 00:18:59,860
So I give the model
the answer, and then I

417
00:18:59,860 --> 00:19:01,360
ask you to produce
the rationale.

418
00:19:01,360 --> 00:19:04,380
So, for example, the answer
is 42 the human says.

419
00:19:04,380 --> 00:19:06,520
And then the model
generates the reasoning.

420
00:19:06,520 --> 00:19:11,060
And then you can fine tune the
model on, again, the problem

421
00:19:11,060 --> 00:19:14,860
rationale and the answer,
but don't show it the hint as

422
00:19:14,860 --> 00:19:16,360
if it was solved directly.

423
00:19:16,360 --> 00:19:18,900
So this helps you expand
the training set to include

424
00:19:18,900 --> 00:19:20,780
more difficult problems now.

425
00:19:20,780 --> 00:19:23,820
So basically, it
allows you to bootstrap

426
00:19:23,820 --> 00:19:26,620
the reasoning
capabilities and not just,

427
00:19:26,620 --> 00:19:29,420
say, limited to easy
problems that the model

428
00:19:29,420 --> 00:19:32,800
could solve earlier, but now you
can bootstrap it iteratively.

429
00:19:32,800 --> 00:19:34,880
So you basically generate
reasoning attempts.

430
00:19:34,880 --> 00:19:36,800
You learn from successful
reasoning paths

431
00:19:36,800 --> 00:19:38,467
where the quality of
the reasoning paths

432
00:19:38,467 --> 00:19:41,080
is entirely based on
things being correct.

433
00:19:41,080 --> 00:19:45,340
And for failed attempts, you're
basically rationalizing them

434
00:19:45,340 --> 00:19:47,340
by saying, OK, here is a hint.

435
00:19:47,340 --> 00:19:48,940
Please generate the rationale.

436
00:19:48,940 --> 00:19:51,220
And then you're using
these generated rationales

437
00:19:51,220 --> 00:19:52,200
as the training data.

438
00:19:52,200 --> 00:19:55,040
So that's basically the
algorithm that they use in STaR.

439
00:19:55,040 --> 00:19:58,020
It's very bare bones,
and like you can almost

440
00:19:58,020 --> 00:20:00,340
call it an off-policy
