00:22:24,670 --> 00:22:27,670
So the problem is
too [INAUDIBLE]

499
00:22:27,670 --> 00:22:29,990
it fails as a pattern.

500
00:22:29,990 --> 00:22:33,550
Even if you tell the
model the right answer,

501
00:22:33,550 --> 00:22:36,070
it's tests do not do
well, because it's just

502
00:22:36,070 --> 00:22:39,310
too complicated to
look at the model.

503
00:22:39,310 --> 00:22:40,650
That's a great question.

504
00:22:40,650 --> 00:22:44,750
So, as I told you in
step four, the assumption

505
00:22:44,750 --> 00:22:48,310
is being made that the benchmark
on which this is being done

506
00:22:48,310 --> 00:22:53,970
is essentially where the
problem is not too difficult.

507
00:22:53,970 --> 00:22:56,790
It is within range, or
some subset of the problems

508
00:22:56,790 --> 00:23:00,070
are within range of
the language model.

509
00:23:00,070 --> 00:23:03,550
And typically you can--

510
00:23:03,550 --> 00:23:06,010
so that is an
assumption that is made.

511
00:23:06,010 --> 00:23:07,550
And then when you
do iteratively,

512
00:23:07,550 --> 00:23:09,670
the model might get
better at certain point

513
00:23:09,670 --> 00:23:13,728
that it can start handling
these other problems.

514
00:23:13,728 --> 00:23:15,970
I have a question in step three.

515
00:23:15,970 --> 00:23:24,750
So in the NLP like
[INAUDIBLE] direction.

516
00:23:24,750 --> 00:23:26,990
So I'm just wondering,
is there a possibility

517
00:23:26,990 --> 00:23:30,710
that you can know from
the step two [INAUDIBLE].

518
00:23:30,710 --> 00:23:41,830
And in step three [INAUDIBLE]
you just use [INAUDIBLE]

519
00:23:41,830 --> 00:23:43,170
So that's a great point.

520
00:23:43,170 --> 00:23:44,490
Let's come back to that one.

521
00:23:44,490 --> 00:23:46,430
So I think what I'll
show you by the end

522
00:23:46,430 --> 00:23:49,190
of the lecture is that
learning from negative examples

523
00:23:49,190 --> 00:23:50,650
has not been nailed.

524
00:23:50,650 --> 00:23:52,910
Learning from positive
examples has been.

525
00:23:52,910 --> 00:23:54,930
Like, if you have
any non-zero reward,

526
00:23:54,930 --> 00:23:58,990
then you have an ability to
close the reinforcement learning

527
00:23:58,990 --> 00:24:01,790
loop, which is not happening
yet in this particular set

528
00:24:01,790 --> 00:24:02,870
of examples.

529
00:24:02,870 --> 00:24:07,710
So you do need to be able to
close the loop in some way.

530
00:24:07,710 --> 00:24:10,358
So if you have
negative examples,

531
00:24:10,358 --> 00:24:12,650
there are some papers that
have tried to learn from it,

532
00:24:12,650 --> 00:24:16,870
but that's not something
we'll cover today.

533
00:24:16,870 --> 00:24:17,390
Yes.

534
00:24:17,390 --> 00:24:19,110
I have a similar
question worrying

535
00:24:19,110 --> 00:24:22,030
how to evaluate or
verify the quality

536
00:24:22,030 --> 00:24:26,670
of the generated rationales
on this failed attempts

537
00:24:26,670 --> 00:24:29,950
after giving the hints,
like since the model already

538
00:24:29,950 --> 00:24:36,270
know the answer, but it might
generate incorrect reasoning

539
00:24:36,270 --> 00:24:37,990
that leads to [INAUDIBLE].

540
00:24:37,990 --> 00:24:39,990
So that's a great problem too.

541
00:24:39,990 --> 00:24:43,470
So you're saying
that in step three

542
00:24:43,470 --> 00:24:45,418
it might generate
wrong rationales is

543
00:24:45,418 --> 00:24:46,210
what you're saying?

544
00:24:46,210 --> 00:24:47,190
Yeah.

545
00:24:47,190 --> 00:24:51,170
Then it will learn wrong
reasoning from correct attempts.

546
00:24:51,170 --> 00:24:53,470
Yes, that will be a problem.

547
00:24:53,470 --> 00:24:57,750
In this particular case, they
don't have any further filtering

548
00:24:57,750 --> 00:25:00,990
on top of rationales.

549
00:25:00,990 --> 00:25:04,550
So there would be no way
to hill climb in that case.

550
00:25:04,550 --> 00:25:08,790
You can have humans look
at the reasoning chains.

551
00:25:08,790 --> 00:25:10,110
It could be tedious, yes.

552
00:25:13,160 --> 00:25:13,760
OK.

553
00:25:13,760 --> 00:25:17,500
So going through this in a
little bit more rigorous detail.

554
00:25:17,500 --> 00:25:19,080
So you start with
a prompt data set

555
00:25:19,080 --> 00:25:22,060
where you have the questions,
the rationales, and the answers.

556
00:25:22,060 --> 00:25:24,560
This prompt data
set will be small,

557
00:25:24,560 --> 00:25:29,100
and compare it to the amount of
data that we want to train on.

558
00:25:29,100 --> 00:25:32,210
For example, this is the
multiple choice questions.

559
00:25:32,210 --> 00:25:34,460
Where do you put your grapes
just before checking out?

560
00:25:34,460 --> 00:25:36,520
There's a rationale, and
then the correct answer

561
00:25:36,520 --> 00:25:39,560
is for example B,
which is grocery cart.

562
00:25:39,560 --> 00:25:41,320
Now, you have a
training data set

563
00:25:41,320 --> 00:25:45,180
which is quite large of examples
that have questions and answers.

564
00:25:45,180 --> 00:25:47,660
This would be, say, if
you have a benchmark,

565
00:25:47,660 --> 00:25:50,760
you might have a trained subset
of the benchmark and a test

566
00:25:50,760 --> 00:25:52,440
subset of the benchmark.

567
00:25:52,440 --> 00:25:54,440
And what Vanilla
STaR will do is it

568
00:25:54,440 --> 00:25:57,480
will start with this rationale
data set and training data set.

569
00:25:57,480 --> 00:26:00,880
So it will first few-shot prompt
the model with the rationales

570
00:26:00,880 --> 00:26:02,400
in the Rationale data set.

571
00:26:02,400 --> 00:26:06,880
And then it will get the model
to produce the rationales

572
00:26:06,880 --> 00:26:09,160
and the correct output.

573
00:26:09,160 --> 00:26:10,660
So this is on the
training data set.

574
00:26:10,660 --> 00:26:15,880
So now it's few-shot prompting,
but it's supplying questions

575
00:26:15,880 --> 00:26:17,440
from the training
data set, and it's

576
00:26:17,440 --> 00:26:20,560
checking if the answers are
correct by the language model.

577
00:26:20,560 --> 00:26:25,280
And it will collect these set
of rationales that it can use,

578
00:26:25,280 --> 00:26:27,400
and then it can fine
tune the language model

579
00:26:27,400 --> 00:26:29,160
on these correct solutions.

580
00:26:29,160 --> 00:26:33,760
If you have a rationalization,
which is what STaR does,

581
00:26:33,760 --> 00:26:36,680
if the answer is
incorrect, what you will do

582
00:26:36,680 --> 00:26:37,900
is you will add hint.

583
00:26:37,900 --> 00:26:40,640
So you'll be saying, OK,
here is the correct answer,

584
00:26:40,640 --> 00:26:43,880
and you will essentially
generate the rationale

585
00:26:43,880 --> 00:26:45,400
by looking at this
hint, and then

586
00:26:45,400 --> 00:26:49,120
you will use that to add to
that data set of rationales

587
00:26:49,120 --> 00:26:50,700
that led to the correct answer.

588
00:26:50,700 --> 00:26:53,840
And then you can fine tune on
top of that language model.

589
00:26:53,840 --> 00:26:57,160
And so you basically have
these two sets of rationales

590
00:26:57,160 --> 00:27:00,093
that then you can fine tune
on top of this language model,

591
00:27:00,093 --> 00:27:01,760
and you can do this
process iteratively.

592
00:27:01,760 --> 00:27:03,760
So you don't do it just once.

593
00:27:03,760 --> 00:27:04,980
You fine tune it.

594
00:27:04,980 --> 00:27:06,400
Now the language
model perhaps is

595
00:27:06,400 --> 00:27:08,100
able to produce better
reasoning chains.

596
00:27:08,100 --> 00:27:10,600
So now you can, again, start
with your training data set,

597
00:27:10,600 --> 00:27:14,680
and whatever was not correct,
you can redo this process.

598
00:27:14,680 --> 00:27:17,780
So their experiments
were on a small model,

599
00:27:17,780 --> 00:27:20,400
which is an open source
of GPT-3, a 6 billion

600
00:27:20,400 --> 00:27:24,600
parameter model called GPT-J.
They had a pretty small warm-up,

601
00:27:24,600 --> 00:27:26,580
and then they kept a
constant learning rate,

602
00:27:26,580 --> 00:27:29,120
and then they basically had
certain number of iterations

603
00:27:29,120 --> 00:27:30,160
of the outer loop.

604
00:27:30,160 --> 00:27:33,360
And then they
increased the number

605
00:27:33,360 --> 00:27:35,800
of steps in the inner
loop on each step.

606
00:27:35,800 --> 00:27:38,120
So one of the comments
the paper makes

607
00:27:38,120 --> 00:27:42,360
is that they want to have a
slower start in the training

608
00:27:42,360 --> 00:27:44,720
time, and then they
want to slowly increase

609
00:27:44,720 --> 00:27:49,120
the number of things that are
happening in the inner loop.

610
00:27:49,120 --> 00:27:53,240
The data sets that they
tried this on was GSM8K.

611
00:27:53,240 --> 00:27:55,973
So GSM8K is just math
problems that you

612
00:27:55,973 --> 00:27:58,640
have seen where you're basically
asking-- this is a grade school

613
00:27:58,640 --> 00:27:59,340
math problems.

614
00:27:59,340 --> 00:28:01,000
There are about 9k samples.

615
00:28:01,000 --> 00:28:02,260
Then CommonsenseQA.

616
00:28:02,260 --> 00:28:05,370
CommonsenseQA is
essentially problems

617
00:28:05,370 --> 00:28:09,368
that you would see on, say,
related to day-to-day stuff.

618
00:28:09,368 --> 00:28:11,410
So you will have these
multiple-choice questions,

619
00:28:11,410 --> 00:28:13,930
and then you have these answers,
and there's rationalization

620
00:28:13,930 --> 00:28:15,110
that looks like this.

621
00:28:15,110 --> 00:28:16,930
And then arithmetic
problems, which

622
00:28:16,930 --> 00:28:19,050
basically was some sort
of an addition problem

623
00:28:19,050 --> 00:28:22,050
on multi-digit addition.

624
00:28:22,050 --> 00:28:25,370
So they generate the last
one was synthetic data

625
00:28:25,370 --> 00:28:30,810
where like they generated some
of these things themselves.

626
00:28:30,810 --> 00:28:33,850
So in terms of results,
some of the results

627
00:28:33,850 --> 00:28:36,850
that stood out in this
paper was that just compared

628
00:28:36,850 --> 00:28:39,630
to, say, supervised fine
tuning on, say, math benchmark,

629
00:28:39,630 --> 00:28:44,270
they could boost with this
STaR approach to 51.7%,

630
00:28:44,270 --> 00:28:48,690
but they only use 70% to 87%
of the data in terms of--

631
00:28:48,690 --> 00:28:50,850
so they had to throw out
a whole bunch of data that

632
00:28:50,850 --> 00:28:52,810
did not have correct solutions.

633
00:28:52,810 --> 00:28:56,830
And other challenge is that
it's not really true RL.

634
00:28:56,830 --> 00:28:59,950
So if you basically do
multiple iterations of this,

635
00:28:59,950 --> 00:29:01,830
it starts to plateau
after a while.

636
00:29:01,830 --> 00:29:05,790
So, I mean, RL has its
own set of challenges,

637
00:29:05,790 --> 00:29:07,830
but here it also
starts to plateau.

638
00:29:07,830 --> 00:29:09,810
So you have to
really pay attention

639
00:29:09,810 --> 00:29:11,872
to how many
iterations you can do.

640
00:29:11,872 --> 00:29:13,330
And there's some
amount of tweaking

641
00:29:13,330 --> 00:29:16,070
there that you have to do.

642
00:29:16,070 --> 00:29:17,570
The other interesting
set of results

643
00:29:17,570 --> 00:29:22,090
here were that this was on
a common sense reasoning.

644
00:29:22,090 --> 00:29:24,530
So they actually looked at--

645
00:29:24,530 --> 00:29:27,350
this was a question that
several folks asked here.

646
00:29:27,350 --> 00:29:30,610
They actually tried to see the
rationales for CommonsenseQA.

647
00:29:30,610 --> 00:29:34,010
They showed it to human raters,
and they asked human raters

648
00:29:34,010 --> 00:29:39,570
to say how likely they are to
prefer the rationales from STaR

649
00:29:39,570 --> 00:29:43,110
versus just like otherwise.

650
00:29:43,110 --> 00:29:46,370
So STaR generated rationales
versus the few-shot rationales

651
00:29:46,370 --> 00:29:49,690
in the Rationale data set.

652
00:29:49,690 --> 00:29:50,750
They compared those.

653
00:29:50,750 --> 00:29:53,650
And what they found
was that generally,

654
00:29:53,650 --> 00:29:57,250
the qualitative analysis showed
that their rationales were

655
00:29:57,250 --> 00:29:58,150
pretty reasonable.

656
00:29:58,150 --> 00:29:59,790
Now, CommonsenseQA,
as you remember,

657
00:29:59,790 --> 00:30:04,630
is a natural language like it's
tackling daily problems here.

658
00:30:04,630 --> 00:30:08,090
So as a result of
that, the rationale

659
00:30:08,090 --> 00:30:09,570
seemed pretty reasonable.

660
00:30:09,570 --> 00:30:12,170
And then the other
aspect that they noticed

661
00:30:12,170 --> 00:30:15,250
was that STaR, in
general, requires--

662
00:30:15,250 --> 00:30:16,950
like, if you use
direct fine tuning,

663
00:30:16,950 --> 00:30:19,430
you basically do get a
certain level of accuracy.

664
00:30:19,430 --> 00:30:21,910
But STaR with rationalization
gets that accuracy,

665
00:30:21,910 --> 00:30:26,770
but it requires much less
data compared to what

666
00:30:26,770 --> 00:30:28,390
you would need for fine tuning.

667
00:30:28,390 --> 00:30:33,410
So rationalization helps
you get to an accuracy using

668
00:30:33,410 --> 00:30:36,110
like-- so 86% of
the data is used,

669
00:30:36,110 --> 00:30:40,610
but you can basically get to
the deficit accuracy of 72.5%

670
00:30:40,610 --> 00:30:43,230
compared to using a
lot of training data.

671
00:30:43,230 --> 00:30:44,912
So this was a very small model.

672
00:30:44,912 --> 00:30:46,370
So this was the
first signs of life

673
00:30:46,370 --> 00:30:48,770
that was shown for reasoning.

674
00:30:48,770 --> 00:30:50,750
In terms of
mathematical reasoning,

675
00:30:50,750 --> 00:30:53,370
this was an interesting
one on GSM8K.

676
00:30:53,370 --> 00:30:55,570
What they found was that
the use of rationalization

677
00:30:55,570 --> 00:30:57,310
actually did not
improve performance.

678
00:30:57,310 --> 00:31:01,570
So like STaR by itself was not
helping in this particular case.

679
00:31:01,570 --> 00:31:05,490
So what you see is that
you have GPT-J like,

680
00:31:05,490 --> 00:31:07,990
basically, it improves
over the baselines,

681
00:31:07,990 --> 00:31:10,890
but it's basically
not helping too much.

682
00:31:10,890 --> 00:31:14,870
If you directly do fine
tuning on good data,

683
00:31:14,870 --> 00:31:17,010
then you can actually
improve it similarly.

684
00:31:17,010 --> 00:31:18,690
So one of the reasons
that is the case

685
00:31:18,690 --> 00:31:20,370
is the number of
calculation steps

686
00:31:20,370 --> 00:31:23,030
that were used by
the model themselves.

687
00:31:23,030 --> 00:31:25,890
If you force the model to
use chain-of-thought versus

688
00:31:25,890 --> 00:31:28,010
you provide these
rationalization steps

689
00:31:28,010 --> 00:31:29,630
is actually pretty similar.

690
00:31:29,630 --> 00:31:32,630
So in some ways,
it was not helping.

691
00:31:32,630 --> 00:31:34,730
If the problem is too simple.

692
00:31:34,730 --> 00:31:36,730
Like, the whole notion
of getting the model

693
00:31:36,730 --> 00:31:39,590
to reason if the
problem is too simple

694
00:31:39,590 --> 00:31:40,870
was basically not helping.

695
00:31:40,870 --> 00:31:44,730
So GSM8K for GPT-4o was
very much within capability.

696
00:31:44,730 --> 00:31:49,370
So similarly for GPT-J, so there
was not much improvement here

697
00:31:49,370 --> 00:31:50,130
relative too.

698
00:31:50,130 --> 00:31:52,730
But it did show that direct
fine tuned versus STaR

699
00:31:52,730 --> 00:31:56,610
with rationalization you
still had some gains.

700
00:31:56,610 --> 00:32:01,260
So some takeaways here
are that the whole notion

701
00:32:01,260 --> 00:32:03,198
of rationalization
conditions on the fact

702
00:32:03,198 --> 00:32:04,740
that you have an
answer, and then you

703
00:32:04,740 --> 00:32:09,300
can essentially figure out
what rationales would be good.

704
00:32:09,300 --> 00:32:12,700
And the outputs
without rationalization

705
00:32:12,700 --> 00:32:15,740
are examples where the model
is already quite confident.

706
00:32:15,740 --> 00:32:21,280
But then if you can look at the
answer, then in certain cases,

707
00:32:21,280 --> 00:32:24,460
it might actually do a better
job producing the rationale.

708
00:32:24,460 --> 00:32:26,280
And if you do
few-shot prompting,

709
00:32:26,280 --> 00:32:30,380
you can basically really
help the model in how

710
00:32:30,380 --> 00:32:31,980
it should produce rationale.

711
00:32:31,980 --> 00:32:36,220
But the style of
rationale might actually

712
00:32:36,220 --> 00:32:38,620
influence the model
in certain ways.

713
00:32:38,620 --> 00:32:41,420
Like prompt engineering,
in this particular case,

714
00:32:41,420 --> 00:32:43,780
might bias the model
in certain ways.

715
00:32:43,780 --> 00:32:46,320
So effectively there
are certain challenges.

716
00:32:46,320 --> 00:32:50,660
One challenge is that the
rationalization the quality

717
00:32:50,660 --> 00:32:51,920
has certain amount of effects.

718
00:32:51,920 --> 00:32:54,378
And the second bit is that if
your few shot prompting, then

719
00:32:54,378 --> 00:32:57,340
you are biased by how the
rationales were formatted

720
00:32:57,340 --> 00:33:00,140
in your few shot
prompts, and that

721
00:33:00,140 --> 00:33:02,180
might affect what
kind of rationales

722
00:33:02,180 --> 00:33:06,140
the model would produce and
what you're training on.

723
00:33:06,140 --> 00:33:08,860
So, overall, I think,
this kind of gives us

724
00:33:08,860 --> 00:33:12,800
a sense of just that
in the model space,

725
00:33:12,800 --> 00:33:15,520
if you basically bootstrap the
model to generate rationales,

726
00:33:15,520 --> 00:33:17,340
you can get high
sample efficiency

727
00:33:17,340 --> 00:33:20,900
while not having to get humans
to generate these reasoning

728
00:33:20,900 --> 00:33:21,620
chains.

729
00:33:21,620 --> 00:33:23,420
And you can apply
it to many problems.

730
00:33:23,420 --> 00:33:25,280
Like you can apply it
to symbolic problems,

731
00:33:25,280 --> 00:33:27,700
natural language,
mathematical reasoning.

732
00:33:27,700 --> 00:33:31,020
Some of the challenges that are
there in this overall techniques

733
00:33:31,020 --> 00:33:35,340
is that you don't have a very
good evaluation for rationales

734
00:33:35,340 --> 00:33:37,900
for most tasks,
because you basically

735
00:33:37,900 --> 00:33:39,502
have to have a
human evaluate them,

736
00:33:39,502 --> 00:33:41,460
or you need some sort of
a process-reward model

737
00:33:41,460 --> 00:33:42,980
to evaluate them.

738
00:33:42,980 --> 00:33:46,557
There might be true negatives
and false positives.

739
00:33:46,557 --> 00:33:48,640
So you're just filtering
based on correct answers,

740
00:33:48,640 --> 00:33:50,660
so you might have
some invalid steps

741
00:33:50,660 --> 00:33:52,720
in between which
might cause problems.

742
00:33:52,720 --> 00:33:54,300
Yes.

743
00:33:54,300 --> 00:34:01,180
So this method is useful
for frontier models, right?

744
